In the board under test, the isolated copper pad at (284,244) is joined to the main trace by a thin copper bridge running from about x=237 to x=275. The golden reference has no such bridge, so this is a short between the pad and the trace.

There are also small pinholes and specks, but the short is the worst defect.

{"defect_type":"short","severity":"critical","disposition":"scrap","confidence":0.85,"reason":"A thin copper bridge connects the isolated round pad at (284,244) to the main trace, which is absent in the golden reference, creating an unintended short."}

The board under test matches the golden reference closely. The only visible difference is a tiny speck near (542, 237) inside the right copper trace. It is a very small pinhole, barely a pixel or two across, and it does not threaten trace continuity.

Under the disposition policy, a pinhole requires rework.

{"defect_type":"pinhole","severity":"cosmetic","disposition":"rework","confidence":0.6,"reason":"A tiny isolated void appears in the right copper trace near (542,237) that is absent in the golden reference, with no impact on trace continuity."}

The board under test has thin traces bridging separate copper features that are isolated in the golden reference. One runs from the trace's lower edge down to the small pad at (281, 460). Another runs from the right-hand pad at about (580, 410) to the large circle at (545, 430). Both connect copper that should be isolated, so they are shorts. There are also small pinholes in the large circles and minor spurs along the trace.

The shorts are the worst defect, so the board is scrapped.

{"defect_type":"short","severity":"critical","disposition":"scrap","confidence":0.9,"reason":"Thin copper bridges connect the trace to the small pad below it and the right-hand pad to the large circular pad, joining features that are isolated in the golden reference."}
